  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nIsolated showers and storms are currently ongoing across the area \nand will persist until around 10 PM tonight. No severe weather is \nexpected. Any areas that see a decent amount of rainfall today will \nlikely see fog tonight. \n\nTomorrow, showers and storms are once again expected during the \nafternoon and evening hours, but coverage should be a little greater \nthan today. This is due to the positioning of the high for tomorrow, \nwhich will be just west of our area. This puts us on the eastern \nperiphery which means we will be in \"Ring Of Fire\" so to speak. \nBasically meaning, greater chances for showers and storms due to \nweak impulses moving through the northerly flow and into our area. \nWe remain in a marginal risk for severe weather for tomorrow from \nSPC. The greatest threat will be damaging winds, possible straight \nline and/or downburst. The wind threat is due to good low-level \nlapse rates of around 7 to 8 deg C/km, DCAPE of around 1000 to 1200 \nJ/kg, and drier air at surface which shows up in the soundings as an \ninverted V. Storms will likely begin to develop between 1 and 2 PM \nand dissipate by 10 PM. Again, a few strong to severe storms are \npossible.\n\nFor Sunday, almost the exact same setup as Saturday, though perhaps \na little less coverage of showers and storms. SPC only has our area \nin the general thunder outlook but I wouldn't be surprised to see us \nupgraded to a marginal. \n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, showers and storms remain in the forecast as \nthe ridge shifts further west and the flow becomes more zonal. A \nmore zonal flow provides better chances for at least some isolated \nconvection, less subsidence. Temps and heat indices remain elevated.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, an upper trough is set to swing \nthrough the area. In addition to increased chances for showers and \nstorms, temperatures will begin cooling off behind on the backside \nof the trough. Drier and cooler conditions expected on Thursday and \nFriday. High temperatures will be in the 70s/80s as opposed to the \n80s/90s. Overnight lows will be in the 50s/60s instead of the \n60s/70s. So hold on for a few more days of heat, relief is on the \nway! \n\nLastly, until we see a relief in temps, Chattanooga will continue to \nbuild onto their record streak of 90 degrees or higher. They \ncurrently sit at 45 days, with several more days likely to add to \nthe streak. The first official day where the forecast is below 90 is \nnext Wednesday, which a high of 87.",
